{"backgroundInformation":"This Flight Simulator corresponds to exercise 7.6 of the course book of the Introductory System Dynamics courses of Delft University of Technology: Pruyt, E. et al. 2009. Exercises Continuous Systems Modelling II: System Dynamics. SPM2313. Delft University of Technology, Delft, The Netherlands. pp121. This hot teaching/testing case/model and a more detailed policy model on share price signaled bank runs are published (in a slightly different form) in: Pruyt, E. (2009). Saving a Bank? Cracking the Case of the Fortis Bank? In: Proceedings of the 27th International Conference of the System Dynamics Society, Albuquerque, USA. CASE DESCRIPTION: The recent credit crunch, which started as a sub-prime mortgage crisis in the United States, also caused serious problems for many European banks. One of the biggest banks of the Benelux (Belgium, the Netherlands and Luxembourg), the Fortis Bank, almost went bankrupt. The governments of the three countries met in a great hurry in the weekend of September 27-28 2008 to rescue the Fortis Bank. The reason for rescuing the bank at all cost is that the Fortis Bank is a system bank, which means that its collapse leads to a collapse of the rest of the financial system. So let us go back to the weekend of September 27-28, let us ignore what happened afterwards, and assume that you are asked by the three ministers of finance to help them really understand the issue at hand and help them design an appropriate policy to prevent the Fortis Bank from going bankrupt. Several top-level financial/banking experts and government officials are able to provide you with some general, high-level information, and more specific details concerning the Fortis Bank [these details and the data used in this educational simulation are fictitious]: The stock market value of the Fortis company can only increase by means of an inflow –the stock market value increase– and can only decrease by means of an outflow –the stock market value loss. Generally speaking, the stock market value loss equals the relative perceived overvaluation times the stock market value divided by the market loss delay of about 1 week. And the stock market value increase equals the perceived market opportunity times the stock market value divided by a market increase delay of about 4 weeks. Suppose that the initial stock market value of the Fortis Bank, some 10 weeks before the critical week, amounted to EURO24.6. The perceived overvaluation –in relative terms, thus expressed in percentages– is then nothing but the difference between the stock market value and the estimated underlying value, divided by the stock market value. One of the financial experts advices you to model the perceived market opportunity as: (100% - instant market fear and uncertainty) * perceived undervaluation. The perceived undervaluation –in relative terms, thus expressed as a percentage– is then nothing but the difference between the estimated underlying asset value and the stock market value, divided by the stock market value. Suppose that the estimated underlying value (per share) of the Fortis Bank amounted initially, some 10 weeks before the catastrophic week, to EURO25. The estimated underlying value can increase by means of underlying asset increases and can decrease by means of underlying asset losses (e.g. in case of a run on the bank, loss of unsecured assets, etc). The underlying asset increase could be simplified as the average profitability on the underlying assets times the estimated underlying asset value. The average profitability of the assets amounts to 0.2% per week. The financial advisers agree that the underlying asset loss should best be modeled as: underlying asset loss = external real value loss shock * estimated underlying value + (average bank client’s fear and uncertainty * 1/stickiness assets) * estimated underlying value. [The assumption behind this formulation is off course that when fear and uncertainty increase, average bank depositors start to empty their deposits and average lenders stop lending, which needs to be compensated from the bank's assets, liquid at first but more and more fixed (by liquidating them). For a better formulation, see the policy model]. The external real value loss shock should be used to model and simulate the asset shock as a percentage of the estimated underlying value. This external real value loss shock amounted in week 10 to 35% in this 1 week time. . . The exact average stickiness of the assets of the Fortis Bank is unknown: it could lie anywhere between 4 weeks and 52 weeks. Just take the average value of 28 weeks for a start. The average bank client’s fear and uncertainty follows the market fear and uncertainty, but then smoothed. Model the average bank client’s fear and uncertainty therefore as a third order delay of the instant market fear and uncertainty with an uncertainty and fear delay of 2 weeks. Initially, the average bank client’s fear and uncertainty is equal to 0%. The instant market fear and uncertainty could be modeled as: instant market fear and uncertainty = degree of external climate of fear * stock market value loss / (stock market value / market loss delay ¤ stock market fear factor ). The instant market fear and uncertainty should also remain between 0% (absence of uncertainty and fear) and 100% (complete uncertainty and fear). The stock market fear factor is assumed to be some 25%. This factor is used to put the loss into perspective – that is to say, relative to the share price: the 25% means that people freak out completely if the share price drops 25% of its initial value during the market loss delay period of one week. Since the general climate is one of uncertainty and fear, the degree of external climate of fear could be assumed to be rather high, for example twice as high as before the crisis, thus 200%.\nCASE QUESTIONS: Using this description, students are asked to make a System Dynamics simulation model of this issue, to verify the model, to include additional formulas/code/structures to ensure that variables that cannot become negative indeed remain greater or equal to zero, to validate the model, to simulate the model over a period of 52 weeks, to make graphs for several variables, to test the sensitivity of the model for particularly uncertain variables, to test base case and worst case scenarios, to draw an extremely aggregated/simple causal loop diagram to communicate the main feedback effects responsible for the worst case system behavior, to explain the link between structure & behavior briefly for the worst case scenario, and to add a simple closed loop policy to prevent the bank from collapsing, no matter which of the scenarios actually materializes.","@type":"XMILEModel","modelId":"Saving_a_Bank","sourceFile":"Saving_a_Bank.xmile","simSpecs":{"method":"RK4","time_units":"Week","start":"0","stop":"52","dt":"0.0078125"},"variables":[{"type":"stock","name":"estimated_underlying_asset_value","units":"EURO","eqn":"initial_estimated_underlying_asset_value","inflows":["underlying asset increase"],"outflows":["underlying asset loss"]},{"type":"stock","name":"stock_market_value","units":"EURO","eqn":"initial_stock_market_value","inflows":["stock market value increase"],"outflows":["stock market value loss"]},{"type":"aux","name":"average_bank_client's_fear_and_uncertainty","units":"Dmnl","eqn":"smoothed_market_fear_and_uncertainty"},{"type":"aux","name":"average_stickiness_of_deposits_and_loans","units":"Week","eqn":"GAME ( 4)"},{"type":"aux","name":"degree_of_external_climate_of_fear","units":"Dmnl","eqn":"GAME ( 2)"},{"type":"aux","name":"external_shock_real_value_loss","units":"1/Week","eqn":"STEP(size_of_the_external_underlying_asset_shock,10) - STEP(size_of_the_external_underlying_asset_shock,11)"},{"type":"aux","name":"financial_intervention","units":"EURO/Week","eqn":"MAX(0,(fraction_initial_guaranteed*initial_estimated_underlying_asset_value - estimated_underlying_asset_value)/action_delay) * policy_switch"},{"type":"aux","name":"fraction_initial_guaranteed","units":"Dmnl","eqn":"GAME ( 0.5)"},{"type":"aux","name":"instant_market_fear_and_uncertainty","units":"Dmnl","eqn":"MIN(stock_market_value_loss/(stock_market_value/market_loss_delay*stock_market_fear_factor) * degree_of_external_climate_of_fear,1)"},{"type":"aux","name":"liquid_deposits_and_loans_loss_fraction","units":"","eqn":"average_bank_client's_fear_and_uncertainty/average_stickiness_of_deposits_and_loans"},{"type":"aux","name":"perceived_overvaluation","units":"Dmnl","eqn":"MAX((stock_market_value-estimated_underlying_asset_value)/stock_market_value,0)"},{"type":"aux","name":"perceived_undervaluation","units":"Dmnl","eqn":"MAX((estimated_underlying_asset_value-stock_market_value)/stock_market_value,0)"},{"type":"aux","name":"policy_switch","units":"Dmnl","eqn":"GAME ( 0)"},{"type":"aux","name":"size_of_the_external_underlying_asset_shock","units":"Dmnl","eqn":"GAME ( 0.35)"},{"type":"aux","name":"smoothed_market_fear_and_uncertainty","units":"Dmnl","eqn":"DELAY3I(instant_market_fear_and_uncertainty, uncertainty_and_fear_delay, initial_market_fear_and_uncertainty)"},{"type":"aux","name":"stock_market_fear_factor","units":"Dmnl","eqn":"GAME ( 0.25)"},{"type":"aux","name":"stock_market_value_increase","units":"EURO/Week","eqn":"MAX(stock_market_value * perceived_undervaluation/market_increase_delay,0)"},{"type":"aux","name":"stock_market_value_loss","units":"EURO/Week","eqn":"MAX(stock_market_value * (perceived_overvaluation/market_loss_delay),0)"},{"type":"aux","name":"underlying_asset_increase","units":"EURO/Week","eqn":"average_profitability*estimated_underlying_asset_value + financial_intervention"},{"type":"aux","name":"underlying_asset_loss","units":"EURO/Week","eqn":"MAX((liquid_deposits_and_loans_loss_fraction*fraction_of_asset_loss_to_deposit_loss+external_shock_real_value_loss)*estimated_underlying_asset_value ,0)"},{"type":"aux","name":"action_delay","units":"Week","eqn":"1"},{"type":"aux","name":"average_profitability","units":"1/Week","eqn":"0.002"},{"type":"aux","name":"fraction_of_asset_loss_to_deposit_loss","units":"Dmnl","eqn":"1"},{"type":"aux","name":"initial_estimated_underlying_asset_value","units":"EURO","eqn":"25"},{"type":"aux","name":"initial_market_fear_and_uncertainty","units":"Dmnl","eqn":"0"},{"type":"aux","name":"initial_stock_market_value","units":"EURO","eqn":"24.6"},{"type":"aux","name":"market_increase_delay","units":"Week","eqn":"4"},{"type":"aux","name":"market_loss_delay","units":"Week","eqn":"1"},{"type":"aux","name":"uncertainty_and_fear_delay","units":"Week","eqn":"2"}]}